NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000276 "}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000277 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000278 "1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000279 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000280 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000281 "=encoding utf-8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000282 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000283 "=for stopwords"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000284 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000285 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000287 "Hash::MultiValue - Store multiple values per key"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000289 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000290 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000291 "  use Hash::MultiValue;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000292 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000293 "  my $hash = Hash::MultiValue->new("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000294 "      foo => 'a',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000295 "      foo => 'b',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000296 "      bar => 'baz',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000297 "  );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000298 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000299 "  # $hash is an object, but can be used as a hashref and DWIMs!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000300 "  my $foo = $hash->{foo};         # 'b' (the last entry)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000301 "  my $foo = $hash->get('foo');    # 'b' (always, regardless of context)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000302 "  my @foo = $hash->get_all('foo'); # ('a', 'b')"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000303 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000304 "  keys %$hash; # ('foo', 'bar')    not guaranteed to be ordered"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000305 "  $hash->keys; # ('foo', 'foo', 'bar') guaranteed to be ordered"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000307 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000309 "Hash::MultiValue is an object (and a plain hash reference) that may"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000310 "contain multiple values per key, inspired by MultiDict of WebOb."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000311 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000312 "=head1 RATIONALE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000313 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000314 "In a typical web application, the request parameters (a.k.a CGI"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000315 "parameters) can be single value or multi values. Using CGI.pm style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000316 "C<param> is one way to deal with this problem (and it is good, as long"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000317 "as you're aware of its list context gotcha), but there's another"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000318 "approach to convert parameters into a hash reference, like Catalyst's"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000319 "C<< $c->req->parameters >> does, and it B<sucks>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000320 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000321 "Why? Because the value could be just a scalar if there is one value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000322 "and an array ref if there are multiple, depending on I<user input>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000323 "rather than I<how you code it>. So your code should always be like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000324 "this to be defensive:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000326 "  my $p = $c->req->parameters;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000327 "  my @maybe_multi = ref $p->{m} eq 'ARRAY' ? @{$p->{m}} : ($p->{m});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000328 "  my $must_single = ref $p->{m} eq 'ARRAY' ? $p->{m}->[0] : $p->{m};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000329 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000330 "Otherwise you'll get a random runtime exception of I<Can't use string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000331 "as an ARRAY ref> or get stringified array I<ARRAY(0xXXXXXXXXX)> as a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000332 "string, I<depending on user input> and that is miserable and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000333 "insecure."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000335 "This module provides a solution to this by making it behave like a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000336 "single value hash reference, but also has an API to get multiple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000337 "values on demand, explicitly."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000338 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000339 "=head1 HOW THIS WORKS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000340 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000341 "The object returned by C<new> is a blessed hash reference that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000342 "contains the last entry of the same key if there are multiple values,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000343 "but it also keeps the original pair state in the object tracker (a.k.a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000344 "inside out objects) and allows you to access the original pairs and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000345 "multiple values via the method calls, such as C<get_all> or C<flatten>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000346 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000347 "This module does not use C<tie> or L<overload> and is quite fast."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000348 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000349 "Yes, there is L<Tie::Hash::MultiValue> and this module tries to solve"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000350 "exactly the same problem, but using a different implementation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000352 "=head1 UPDATING CONTENTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000354 "When you update the content of the hash, B<DO NOT UPDATE> using the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000355 "hash reference interface: this won't write through to the tracking"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000356 "object."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000357 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000358 "  my $hash = Hash::MultiValue->new(...);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000359 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000360 "  # WRONG"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000361 "  $hash->{foo} = 'bar';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000362 "  delete $hash->{foo};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000364 "  # Correct"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000365 "  $hash->add(foo => 'bar');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000366 "  $hash->remove('foo');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000368 "See below for the list of updating methods."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000369 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000370 "=head1 METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000372 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000374 "=item new"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000376 "  $hash = Hash::MultiValue->new(@pairs);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000378 "Creates a new object that can be treated as a plain hash reference as well."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000379 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000380 "=item get"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000381 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000382 "  $value = $hash->get($key);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000383 "  $value = $hash->{$key};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000384 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000385 "Returns a single value for the given C<$key>. If there are multiple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000386 "values, the last one (not first one) is returned. See below for why."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000387 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000388 "Note that this B<always> returns the single element as a scalar,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000389 "regardless of its context, unlike CGI.pm's C<param> method etc."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000390 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000391 "=item get_one"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000393 "  $value = $hash->get_one($key);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000395 "Returns a single value for the given C<$key>. This method B<croaks> if"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000396 "there is no value or multiple values associated with the key, so you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000397 "should wrap it with eval or modules like L<Try::Tiny>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000398 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000399 "=item get_all"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000400 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000401 "  @values = $hash->get_all($key);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000402 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000403 "Returns a list of values for the given C<$key>. This method B<always>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000404 "returns a list regardless of its context. If there is no value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000405 "attached, the result will be an empty list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000406 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000407 "=item keys"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000408 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000409 "  @keys = $hash->keys;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000411 "Returns a list of all keys, including duplicates (see the example in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000412 "L</SYNOPSIS>)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000414 "If you want only unique keys, use C<< keys %$hash >>, as normal."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000416 "=item values"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000418 "  @values = $hash->values;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000420 "Returns a list of all values, in the same order as C<< $hash->keys >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000422 "=item set"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000424 "  $hash->set($key [, $value ... ]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000426 "Changes the stored value(s) of the given C<$key>. This removes or adds"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000427 "pairs as necessary to store the new list but otherwise preserves order"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000428 "of existing pairs. C<< $hash->{$key} >> is updated to point to the last"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000429 "value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000430 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000431 "=item add"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000432 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000433 "  $hash->add($key, $value [, $value ... ]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000434 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000435 "Appends a new value to the given C<$key>. This updates the value of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000436 "C<< $hash->{$key} >> as well so it always points to the last value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000438 "=item remove"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000439 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000440 "  $hash->remove($key);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000441 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000442 "Removes a key and associated values for the given C<$key>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000443 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000444 "=item clear"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000445 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000446 "  $hash->clear;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000448 "Clears the hash to be an empty hash reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000450 "=item flatten"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000451 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000452 "  @pairs = $hash->flatten;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000454 "Gets pairs of keys and values. This should be exactly the same pairs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000455 "which are given to C<new> method unless you updated the data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000457 "=item each"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000458 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000459 "  $hash->each($code);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000460 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000461 "  # e.g."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000462 "  $hash->each(sub { print \"$_[0] = $_[1]\\n\" });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000464 "Calls C<$code> once for each C<($key, $value)> pair.  This is a more convenient"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000465 "alternative to calling C<flatten> and then iterating over it two items at a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000466 "time."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000468 "Inside C<$code>, C<$_> contains the current iteration through the loop,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000469 "starting at 0.  For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000470 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000471 "  $hash = Hash::MultiValue->new(a => 1, b => 2, c => 3, a => 4);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000472 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000473 "  $hash->each(sub { print \"$_: $_[0] = $_[1]\\n\" });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000474 "  # 0: a = 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000475 "  # 1: b = 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000476 "  # 2: c = 3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000477 "  # 3: a = 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000478 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000479 "Be careful B<not> to change C<@_> inside your coderef!  It will update"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000480 "the tracking object but not the plain hash.  In the future, this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000481 "limitation I<may> be removed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000482 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000483 "=item clone"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000484 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000485 "  $new = $hash->clone;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000486 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000487 "Creates a new Hash::MultiValue object that represents the same data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000488 "but obviously not sharing the reference. It's identical to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000489 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000490 "  $new = Hash::MultiValue->new($hash->flatten);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000491 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000492 "=item as_hashref"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000493 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000494 "  $copy = $hash->as_hashref;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000495 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000496 "Creates a new plain (unblessed) hash reference where a value is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000497 "single scalar. It's identical to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000498 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000499 "  $copy = +{%$hash};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000500 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000501 "=item as_hashref_mixed, mixed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000503 "  $mixed = $hash->as_hashref_mixed;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000504 "  $mixed = $hash->mixed;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000505 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000506 "Creates a new plain (unblessed) hash reference where the value is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000507 "single scalar, or an array ref when there are multiple values for a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000508 "same key. Handy to create a hash reference that is often used in web"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000509 "application frameworks request objects such as L<Catalyst>. Ths method"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000510 "does exactly the opposite of C<from_mixed>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000511 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000512 "=item as_hashref_multi, multi"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000513 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000514 "  $multi = $hash->as_hashref_multi;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000515 "  $multi = $hash->multi;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000516 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000517 "Creates a new plain (unblessed) hash reference where values are all"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000518 "array references, regardless of there are single or multiple values"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000519 "for a same key."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000520 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000521 "=item from_mixed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000522 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000523 "  $hash = Hash::MultiValue->from_mixed({"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000524 "      foo => [ 'a', 'b' ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000525 "      bar => 'c',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000526 "  });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000527 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000528 "Creates a new object out of a hash reference where the value is single"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000529 "or an array ref depending on the number of elements. Handy to convert"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000530 "from those request objects used in web frameworks such as L<Catalyst>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000531 "This method does exactly the opposite of C<as_hashref_mixed>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000532 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000533 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000534 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000535 "=head1 WHY LAST NOT FIRST?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000536 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000537 "You might wonder why this module uses the I<last> value of the same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000538 "key instead of I<first>. There's no strong reasoning on this decision"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000539 "since one is as arbitrary as the other, but this is more consistent to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000540 "what Perl does:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000541 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000542 "  sub x {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000543 "      return ('a', 'b', 'c');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000544 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000545 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000546 "  my $x = x(); # $x = 'c'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000547 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000548 "  my %a = ( a => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000549 "  my %b = ( a => 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000551 "  my %m = (%a, %b); # $m{a} = 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000553 "When perl gets a list in a scalar context it gets the last entry. Also"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000554 "if you merge hashes having a same key, the last one wins."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000555 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000556 "=head1 NOTES ON ref"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000557 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000558 "If you pass this MultiValue hash object to some upstream functions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000559 "that you can't control and does things like:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000560 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000561 "  if (ref $args eq 'HASH') {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000562 "      ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000563 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000564 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000565 "because this is a blessed hash reference it doesn't match and would"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000566 "fail. To avoid that you should call C<as_hashref> to get a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000567 "I<finalized> (= non-blessed) hash reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000568 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000569 "You can also use UNIVERSAL::ref to make it work magically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000570 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000571 "  use UNIVERSAL::ref;    # before loading Hash::MultiValue"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000572 "  use Hash::MultiValue;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000574 "and then all C<ref> calls to Hash::MultiValue objects will return I<HASH>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000576 "=head1 THREAD SAFETY"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000578 "Prior to version 0.09, this module wasn't safe in a threaded"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000579 "environment, including win32 fork() emulation. Versions newer than"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000580 "0.09 is considered thread safe."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000581 ""
